{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>And Jacob took for himself rods of a storax tree.</b> Since they agreed that his wages would be from these, he was permitted to do whatever he could to produce them accordingly. Or perhaps Jacob stipulated that he could do with them whatever he wished, for Laban did not know about this result, and the shepherds would not notice the matter when they saw the rods in the troughs once a year in the days of Nisan, for when the flock conceived in the days of Tishrei he would not place them, so that nothing would remain to Laban except a hoof. And some say (Radak) that in the first year many speckled and spotted ones were born to Jacob by the blessing of HaShem that the angel had shown him, and he placed the rods before those that were his so that they would give birth in their likeness, so that Laban would not say when they were born that it was stolen from him. And this is the meaning of \"in every occasion when the flock conceived, the ones that were bound\" — these are the ones born to him — \"and when the flock conceived late\" — those of Laban — \"he would not place them, and the late-conceiving ones were Laban's,\" which would not give birth speckled, \"and the bound ones\" all of them \"were Jacob's,\" for they would give birth speckled:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The Hebrew יחם / עטף describes the flock's breeding/conceiving; I render יחם as 'conceived' and עטף as 'conceived late' based on the distinction Ramban draws between the two breeding seasons (Nisan vs. Tishrei). The phrase המקשרות/הקשורים ('bound'/'tied') I render literally; it is unclear whether Ramban takes it as a technical term for the marked/banded animals or in some other sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:28.704113931Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}